===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{senseid|en|reduced price}} To sell at a reduced [[price]].
##: {{ux|en|Sales were slow even after the shop '''discounted''' the product.}}
## {{senseid|en|subtract or deduct mathematically}} {{lb|en|rare}} To [[deduct]] from an account, debt, charge, etc.
##: {{ux|en|Merchants sometimes '''discount''' five or six per cent for prompt payment of bills.}}
## {{senseid|en|advisedly omit or disregard}} To disregard or regard as [[unimportant]].
##: {{ux|en|Owing to his reputation, they '''discounted''' his comments.}}
##* {{RQ:Hamilton Metaphysics and Logic
|passage=Of the three opinions, (I '''discount''' Brown's), under this head, one supposes that the law of Causality is a positive affirmation, and a primary fact of thought, incapable of all further analysis.}}
## {{senseid|en|lend against or buy}} To [[lend]] money upon, deducting the discount or allowance for [[interest]].
##: {{usex|en|The banks '''discount''' notes and bills of exchange.}}
##: {{usex|en|The company's directors were chagrined to learn that banks were suddenly refusing to '''discount''' its notes.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1692|author={{w|William Walsh (poet)|William Walsh}}|title=Letter on the present state of the Currency of Great Britain
|passage='''Discount''' only unexceptionable paper.}}
## {{senseid|en|take into consideration beforehand}} To take into consideration beforehand; to anticipate and form conclusions concerning (an event).
##: {{ux|en|The market has dropped, '''discounting''' changes in interest rates.}}
# {{lb|en|psychology|[[transactional analysis]]}} To believe, or act as though one believes, that one's own feelings are more important than the reality of a situation.

